{{distinguish|Antlia II}}
{{Infobox galaxy|name=Antila B|image=Local Group pl.svg|caption=NGC 3109 (which Antila B is a satellite of) is located towards the far left|constellation name=[[Antila]]|ra=09 48 56.1|dec=-25 59 24|group_cluster=NGC 3109 association|type=Dwarf irregular|names=Ant B|epoch=J2000.0}}

'''Antlia B''' (also known as '''Ant B''') is a faint dwarf irregular [[satellite galaxy]] located around 72 kiloparsecs from [[NGC 3109]], a small [[irregular galaxy]] located 4.3 million light years from [[Earth]] at the edge of the [[Local Group|local group]].<ref name=":0">{{cite journal | last1=Sand | first1=D. J. | last2=Spekkens | first2=K. | last3=Crnojević | first3=D. | last4=Hargis | first4=J. R. | last5=Willman | first5=B. | last6=Strader | first6=J. | last7=Grillmair | first7=C. J. | title=Antlia B: A Faint Dwarf Galaxy Member of the NGC 3109 Association | journal=The Astrophysical Journal | date=2015 | volume=812 | issue=1 | pages=L13 | doi=10.1088/2041-8205/812/1/L13 | arxiv=1508.01800 | bibcode=2015ApJ...812L..13S }}</ref> Antila B has a complex mixture of old [[Red giant|red giant branch stars]] over 10 billion years old and young blue stars only a few hundred years old. Despite Antlia B being rich in gas, Antlia B shows no evidence of active star formation.<ref name=":1">{{cite arXiv | last1=Hargis | first1=Jonathan R. | last2=Albers | first2=S. | last3=Crnojević | first3=D. | last4=Sand | first4=D. J. | last5=Weisz | first5=D. R. | last6=Carlin | first6=J. L. | last7=Spekkens | first7=K. | last8=Willman | first8=B. | last9=Peter | first9=A. H. G. | last10=Grillmair | first10=C. J. | last11=Dolphin | first11=A. E. | title=Hubble Space Telescope Imaging of Antlia B: Star Formation History and a New Tip of the Red Giant Branch Distance | date=2019 | class=astro-ph.GA | eprint=1907.07185 }}</ref>

== Stellar population ==
The [[stellar population]] of Antlia B is composed of prominent old, metal-poor [[red giant]] branch stars with ages greater than 10 billion years and young blue stars somewhere between 200-400 million years old.<ref name=":0" /> Despite Antlia B being rich in gas to form new stars, there seems to be no evidence for active star formation within Antlia B.<ref name=":1" />

=== Stellar history ===
The history of [[star formation]] in Antlia B shows that there was relatively constant stellar mass growth for the first ~10-11 billion years of its history and then almost no growth for the last ~2-3 billion years.<ref name=":1" />

== Discovery ==
The discovery of Antila B was from the [[Dark Energy Survey|Dark Energy Camera]] survey (DES).<ref name=":0" />

